1. BLOCK vs FILE vs OBJECT
--------------------------
  Block (RWO)  EBS (AWS), Azure Disk, GCE Persistent Disk
               one node at a time, ONE AZ only, fast; databases
  File (RWX)   EFS (AWS), Azure Files, Filestore (GCP)
               many nodes, multi-AZ, NFS; shared content
  Object       S3, Blob, GCS: NOT mounted as volumes normally; apps
               use the API (via workload identity, Day 47). Best for
               backups, uploads, static assets.


2. THE EBS CSI DRIVER
---------------------
An add-on (we installed it with eksctl). Components:
  ebs-csi-controller (Deployment)  calls AWS APIs: create, attach,
                                   delete, snapshot volumes
  ebs-csi-node (DaemonSet)         formats and mounts on each node
It needs IAM permissions -> we gave it a role (wellKnownPolicies).


3. STORAGECLASS FOR EBS gp3
---------------------------
  apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
  kind: StorageClass
  metadata:
    name: gp3
    annotations:
      storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"
  provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com
  parameters:
    type: gp3
    encrypted: "true"
  reclaimPolicy: Delete
  volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
  allowVolumeExpansion: true

Newer EKS clusters don't mark any StorageClass as default, so a PVC
without storageClassName stays Pending until you create one like this.


4. THE ZONE TRAP
----------------
An EBS volume lives in ONE AZ. A pod using it can only run on nodes in
that AZ.
  - WaitForFirstConsumer: the disk is created in the AZ where the pod
    was scheduled (good).
  - If later no node in that AZ has room (or the node group has no
    nodes there), the pod stays Pending: "volume node affinity
    conflict".
Mitigations: node groups in every AZ; one node group per AZ for
stateful workloads; RWX file storage (EFS) when pods must move freely;
managed databases.


5. SNAPSHOTS AND EXPANSION
--------------------------
  VolumeSnapshotClass + VolumeSnapshot -> EBS snapshot (backup)
  PVC from a snapshot (dataSource) -> restore/clone
  Increase spec.resources.requests.storage -> online resize


6. STATEFUL IN THE CLOUD: THE HONEST ADVICE
-------------------------------------------
Running databases on Kubernetes is possible (StatefulSets, operators,
Week 9). Most teams still use managed databases (RDS, Aurora, Cloud
SQL, Azure Database) for backups, failover, patching. Kubernetes
apps connect to them over the VPC with credentials from a secret store
or IAM auth.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Block = RWO, single AZ; file = RWX, multi-AZ; object = API.
- CSI driver + IAM + StorageClass = dynamic cloud disks.
- WaitForFirstConsumer and AZ-aware node groups avoid zone traps.
